---
import './design-aids.css';
import SketchSheet from './SketchSheet.astro';
import SketchArrow from './SketchArrow.astro';
---
<figure id="json-write-boundary" class="design-aid design-figure paper-surface json-writes wide" aria-labelledby="json-writes-title">
  <span class="plate-number">Illustration · Updating separate offers</span>
  <h3 id="json-writes-title">Two offers inside one row, or one row each</h3>
  <p>Suppose the mug has offers from suppliers S1 and S2. Two requests change those offers’ prices. The outlines show the rows their updates target.</p>
  <div class="alternatives">
    <div>
      <h4>Offers embedded in the product</h4>
      <div class="requests"><span>Change S1’s price</span><span>Change S2’s price</span></div>
      <div class="arrows"><SketchArrow direction="down" /><SketchArrow direction="down" /></div>
      <SketchSheet tone="amber">
        <p class="row-label">products · P7</p>
        <div class="array">
          <p><code>offers</code> · JSON array</p>
          <div class="offer"><span>supplier_id: S1</span><span>price_eur: 12 → 13</span></div>
          <div class="offer"><span>supplier_id: S2</span><span>price_eur: 14 → 15</span></div>
        </div>
      </SketchSheet>
      <p class="consequence">Both updates need a row lock on P7. While one holds its lock, the other cannot update the row.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>Offers stored as related rows</h4>
      <div class="separate-offers">
        <div><p class="request">Change S1’s price</p><SketchArrow direction="down" /><SketchSheet tone="teal"><p class="row-label">offers · first row</p><dl><div><dt>product_id</dt><dd>P7</dd></div><div><dt>supplier_id</dt><dd>S1</dd></div><div><dt>price_eur</dt><dd>12 → 13</dd></div></dl></SketchSheet></div>
        <div><p class="request">Change S2’s price</p><SketchArrow direction="down" /><SketchSheet tone="teal"><p class="row-label">offers · second row</p><dl><div><dt>product_id</dt><dd>P7</dd></div><div><dt>supplier_id</dt><dd>S2</dd></div><div><dt>price_eur</dt><dd>14 → 15</dd></div></dl></SketchSheet></div>
      </div>
      <p class="consequence">The price updates target different offer rows. They do not need the same row lock merely because both offers belong to P7.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>PostgreSQL row-lock boundaries for this example, with one offer per product and supplier. Separate rows avoid this particular conflict; shared resources or other work in the transactions can still make them wait. The diagram does not promise that the whole operations run independently.</figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .json-writes.wide { margin-left:50%; }
  .alternatives { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1.2fr; gap:2rem; margin-top:1.4rem; }
  .alternatives > div { min-width:0; }
  .requests,.arrows { display:flex; justify-content:space-around; gap:.7rem; }
  .requests,.request { font-size:.75rem; text-align:center; }
  .arrows { margin:.2rem 0; }
  .row-label { font:.75rem var(--font-mono); color:var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .array { border-left:1px solid var(--gold); padding-left:.65rem; }
  .array > p { font-size:.75rem; }
  .offer { display:flex; justify-content:space-between; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:.2rem .5rem; padding:.55rem 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-rule); font:.75rem/1.5 var(--font-mono); }
  .separate-offers { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:.8rem; }
  .separate-offers > div { min-width:0; }
  .separate-offers :global(.sketch-arrow) { margin:0 auto; }
  dl { margin:0; }
  dl > div { display:flex; justify-content:space-between; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:.1rem .5rem; padding:.5rem 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-rule); font:.75rem/1.5 var(--font-mono); }
  dd { margin:0; }
  .consequence { font-size:.82rem; margin-top:1rem !important; }
  @media(max-width:760px) { .alternatives { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }
  @media(max-width:430px) { .separate-offers { grid-template-columns:1fr; gap:1rem; } }
</style>
